A||||5||Ed||Front brakes on '87 GT||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||12-13-1999||10:50 PM||||I'm going to replace the front rotors and pads.  Any tips: Seals, bushings, bearings, races; any obvious or subtle things to watch out for?  I've replaced pads before and have two! manuals, but I haven't removed the rotors before.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DJRice||12-14-1999||09:27 AM||||Its pretty straight forward, just make sure that you dont accidentally let the pistons push out of the calipers enough that you cant move slide the rotor between the pads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-14-1999||09:34 PM||||Since you usually have to pull the rotors off to turn them anyway, I carefully inspect the front wheel bearings and repack them with grease at the very least. Inspect the flexible brake hoses, if they don't look perfect, replace them. Inspect and lube, with synthetic high temperature grease, the caliper slider mounting bolts. It's important that these do not stick or bind. I've found that Fiero brakes are pretty easy to work on compared to many other cars.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||10:50 PM||||You can never be too cautious with brakes.  It doesn't matter if you can't go, but it sucks if you can't stop.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-15-1999||07:05 PM||||I use permatex anti sieze on my slides. works good and doesn't wash out.<P>If you've never done wheel bearings on a GM or other car that uses the castle nuts get someone to help you adjust them the first time. You can over tighten or leave these loose very easy. you'll fry the brearings in either case.<P>Also, there are 2 cotter pin holes in the spindle. Don't assume you will use the same hole when things go back together. Use NEW cotters every time you take the nuts off.<P>Make sure the grease is made for disk brakes. DO NOT use drum brake grease. It's made differantly.<P>If the races or bearing show any damage at all, even a tiny bit of visible wear, replace them.<P>Get a bearing driver for the setting the races in the hub. Lisle tool # 12600 will fit all the bearings and seals on Fiero. This tool will not mar the races, and is easy to control for straight driving.<P>To remove the races from the front hubs you have to use a screw driver or punch. be careful not to gouge the inside of the hub.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||12-16-1999||12:41 AM||||Thanks for the tips to date. I haven't done much in the way of car repairs lately. Your comments help my confidence.<P>The manuals are very clear on how to torque the bearings and then back off. I'm using new rotors so the (already set) races will be good. I know about making sure the sliders are free and well lubricated (GM includes grease and new bushings/o-rings with their pads). And I'll double check for pitted bearings.<P>P.S. Regarding the correct wheel bearing tightness: I went to autocross my first Fiero, an '84, some years ago. I almost didn't pass tech inspection because the checker wasn't familiar with the sort of loose setting that GM uses, as pointed out by theogre. (A real mechanic had adjusted them, not me!)    ||||reg||1
